use std
into cloth.amni

Imports: os, binary, gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.motion, gfx.scene3d and spring (the local module spring.amni; its `mquat`, `pos`, `qconj` and `clamp01` are used unqualified). This file is a module with no main. It simulates garment fabric: each garment group has a cage of points that are skin joints, the render mesh is skinned to those joints, and each frame this module moves the points with position-based dynamics (gravity and the model's wind, distance and bend constraints, tethers to the pinned points, a maximum swing from where the body's skin would carry the point, contact with the body's high points near each point and with its nearest limb capsules), then poses each joint with the point's position and the rotation that best matches its neighbours. Rigid and tight fabric is not simulated at all: it stays skinned to the body and costs nothing. Normalize every vector with `linalg::unit3`; rotate vectors with `linalg::qrotate`; points through matrices with `linalg::point`.

Fabric classes are data, built once into a module `var` from this table (struct `Fabric { name byte[>..], sim bool, cell f32, gain f32, swing f32, bend f32, damp f32, grav f32 }`): sim says whether the class is simulated, cell is the cage spacing, a point's largest swing is `min(swing, gain * max(gap - 0.01, 0))` with gap its rest distance from the body, bend is the bend-edge stiffness, damp the velocity kept per substep and grav the share of gravity.

```table fabric_rows Fabric
rigid|false|0|0|0|0|0|0
tight|false|0|0|0|0|0|0
close|true|0.04|2.0|0.04|0.3|0.98|1.0
loose|true|0.025|3.0|0.15|0.15|0.985|1.0
```

Which mesh is which class, and which meshes share a cage (a group), is also data (struct `Wear { mesh byte[>..], group byte[>..], class byte[>..] }`), built once into a module `var` from this table, then extended by the lines of an optional per-model file (each line: mesh, group and class separated by spaces; a line for a mesh already listed replaces its row):

```table wear_rows Wear
skirt|skirt|loose
tank|tank|tight
tank_hem|tank|close
shorts|shorts|tight
shorts_lace|shorts|tight
socks|socks|tight
stripes|socks|tight
gloves|gloves|tight
belt|belt|rigid
buckle|belt|rigid
choker|choker|rigid
plate|choker|rigid
chain|chain|rigid
shoes|shoes|rigid
glasses|glasses|rigid
outfit|outfit|tight
hoodie|hoodie|close
hood|hoodie|close
hood_hem|hoodie|close
pocket|hoodie|close
seam|hoodie|close
collar|hoodie|close
```

Module state: `var npts = 0`, `var item = -1`, `var sk = -1`, `var ready = false`, `var wind = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }` (a force per unit mass the page may set, meters per second squared); per point: `var p_node int[>..]` (global glTF node), `var p_j int[>..]` (4 per point), `var p_w f32[>..]` (4 per point), `var p_md f32[>..]`, `var p_damp f32[>..]`, `var p_grav f32[>..]`, `var p_rest float3[>..]`, `var p_x float3[>..]`, `var p_prev float3[>..]`, `var p_anc float3[>..]`, `var p_q linalg::Quat[>..]`, `var p_im f32[>..]` (0 for pinned points, else 1), `var p_g int[>..]` (the group each point belongs to, counting from 0), `var p_y f32[>..]` (the point's height within its group at rest, 0 at the lowest point and 1 at the highest), `var p_tie int[>..]` (its tether point, -1 for none), `var p_tl f32[>..]` (tether length), `var p_cap int[>..]` (2 per point: its nearest capsules, -1 for none), `var p_hv int[>..]` (2 per point: indices into the contact list, -1 for none); contacts `var h_v int[>..]` (global vertex of the body's "skin" mesh), `var h_p float3[>..]`, `var h_n float3[>..]` (their skinned position and normal this frame); `var ngar = 0` (groups built), `var cl_asset = -1` (set to the asset at the start of `cloth_setup`); neighbours for shape matching as `var nb_first int[>..]` (npts + 1 offsets) and `var nbr int[>..]`; edges `var e_a int[>..]`, `var e_b int[>..]`, `var e_l f32[>..]`, `var e_k f32[>..]`; caps `var c_a int[>..]`, `var c_b int[>..]`, `var c_r0 f32[>..]`, `var c_r1 f32[>..]`; `var pal gfx::mat4[>..]`.

`setup_group(asset int, sim int[:], sim_class int[:], still int[:])` builds one cage shared by the simulated garment nodes in sim (`sim_class[k]` the fabric row of `sim[k]`); `still` lists the group's unsimulated nodes. `nf = gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first`; the group's vertices are those of every primitive of the mesh of every node in sim (vertex v of a primitive is global vertex `prim.vfirst + v`, its floats at `* gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`: position 0..2, normal 3..5, joints 18..21 as floats, weights 22..25; triangle indices are `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst ..]`, local to the primitive). Collect the global vertex list `gv` and each one's class `gc`. Sample: the cell of a vertex is its class's cell; in order, accept a vertex as a cage seed when no seed accepted so far is within that cell of it (plain loop over the seeds). With m seeds, label every group vertex with its nearest seed (`lab`), set each cage point's rest position to the mean of its labelled vertices and its class to the most common class among them. Adjacency: a `bool` list of m * m, set for both orders of every pair of different labels along each triangle edge; structural edges are the pairs i < j with adjacency (stiffness 1); bend edges are the pairs i < k, not adjacent, sharing a neighbour j (loop i, its neighbours j, their neighbours k, deduplicated with a second m * m list), with stiffness the mean of the two points' class bend. Anchor weights: for each cage point, add up its vertices' joint weights into a list of the skin's joint count, first drop the weights of leg joints (the skin joints whose node is thigh_l or thigh_r or lies below one of them in the node tree) unless that would drop all of the point's weight, so fabric hangs from the hips and the legs move it only by contact; take the four largest (index and weight) and normalize them (when they sum to 0 use the skin's joint of the node "pelvis" with weight 1). Gap: the distance from the cage point's rest position to the nearest vertex of the mesh of the asset's "skin" node or of the meshes of the nodes in `still` (bind-space positions, plain loop); remember the nearest skin vertex `ns`. Max swing `md = min(swing, gain * max(gap - 0.01, 0))` from the point's class; damping and gravity share from its class.
Contacts: for each point, among the skin vertices within 0.07 of its rest position, with `n` the unit direction from `ns` to the rest position (the skin vertex normal at `ns` when the gap is below 0.00001), the high point is the vertex with the largest `dot(v - rest, n)` (the body bulging toward the fabric); the point's contacts are `ns` and the high point (one entry when they are the same), stored once each in `h_v` (look a vertex up before adding it) and their indices in `p_hv`. Capsules are linked after they are made (see `cloth_setup`).
Tethers: over the structural edges with their rest lengths, a shortest-path search (Dijkstra; a plain scan for the nearest unsettled point is fine) from all pinned points of this group at once gives every free point its nearest pinned point along the cloth and that path length: `p_tie` and `p_tl`; points no pinned point reaches get -1.
New joints: with `pv = gltf::find_node(asset, "pelvis")` and its inverse bind matrix `ib` (from the skin, as `ibm_of(pv)`), for each cage point k push a `gltf::Node { parent: pv, mesh: -1, skin: -1, children: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, t: linalg::point(ib, C_k), r: mquat(ib), s: v3(1, 1, 1), matrix: gfx::mat4_identity, has_matrix: false, weights: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, name: gltf::Span { 0, 0 } }` (fill any other field with its default). Then rebuild three spans at the end of their arrays: the pelvis's children (`gltf::ints`: its old children, then the new nodes), the skin's joints (`gltf::ints`: old joints, then the new nodes) and its inverse binds (`gltf::matrices`: old ones, then `gfx::translate(-C_k)` per new joint); set `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.count` up by m. Re-skin the group: for each group vertex find its four nearest cage points (keep the best four while looping over all), weights `1 / max(d, 0.0001)^2` normalized, and write joints `old joint count + cage index` (as f32) into floats 18..21 and the weights into 22..25. Append the points (group `ngar` into `p_g`, the rest height share `(y - bot) / max(top - bot, 0.000001)` with `top` and `bot` the group vertices' largest and smallest y into `p_y`, node, anchor joints and weights, max swing, damping, gravity share, rest `C_k`, `p_x`/`p_prev`/`p_anc` the rest, `p_q` identity, `p_im` 0 when md < 0.0001 else 1, tether, contacts, capsules -1 -1) and the edges (rest length the distance between the rest positions; point and tether indices offset by the points already present), then add 1 to `ngar`.

`ibm_of(node int) gfx::mat4` is the node's inverse bind matrix in skin `sk` (`gltf::matrices[inverse_binds.first + k]` for the joint k with `gltf::ints[joints.first + k] == node`), or the identity when it is not a joint.

`cap_r(a int, b int, k f32) float2` measures the body around the segment from joint a to joint b in bind space (positions `pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(x)))`): over the vertices of the asset's "skin" node mesh whose weights on the skin joints of a and b add up to at least 0.6, with `t` the projection onto the segment (skip outside [0, 1]) and `r` the distance from it, collect r for t < 0.35 into `lo` and t > 0.65 into `hi`; return `(min(q(lo), q(hi) * 1.1), q(hi))` with `q` the k quantile (sort ascending with insertion sort, element at `floor(len * k)` clamped; 0.07 when empty).

`cloth_setup(path const byte[:], asset int) int` runs before the avatar is added to the scene. Set `cl_asset = asset`. Build the fabric and wear tables, then read the per-model file with the unqualified `read_file(path, data)` into a `byte[>..]` and apply its lines when that works. Set `sk` like the spring module does (the skin of the "skin" node when it has a mesh and skin, else of the first node with both; return 0 when none). For each distinct group name in wear-row order: its rows whose mesh some asset node carries (the node whose mesh name equals the row's mesh); the simulated ones (class with sim true) go in a list with their class rows, the others in `still`; call `setup_group` when the simulated list is not empty. When any points were made, add the caps for the joint pairs (thigh_l, calf_l), (thigh_r, calf_r), (calf_l, foot_l), (calf_r, foot_r), (upperarm_l, lowerarm_l), (upperarm_r, lowerarm_r), (lowerarm_l, hand_l), (lowerarm_r, hand_r) whose nodes exist, with radii `cap_r(a, b, 0.99)`, and one for (thigh_l, thigh_r) with both radii the second value of `cap_r(pelvis, pelvis, 0.95)` measured along the thigh_l to thigh_r segment (use that segment for t and r, and the pelvis joint's weight for the 0.6 test). Link each point to its two nearest caps by rest distance (bind-space joint positions `pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(x)))`, distance from the segment minus the radius at the closest point of the segment) into `p_cap`, skipping caps the point already lies inside at rest (that distance below 0), since such a cap does not describe the body under it. Build the neighbour lists from the edges with stiffness at least 0.99 (both directions; count per point, prefix sums into nb_first, then fill). Set `npts` and return it.

`cloth_open(it int)` sets `item = it`.

`anchors()` sets `motion::joints(sk, pal)`, calls `contacts()` and then, for every point i, `p_anc[i]` to the sum over its four joints of `p_w[4 i + c] * linalg::point(pal[p_j[4 i + c]], p_rest[i])`.

`contacts()` skins the contact vertices for this frame: for each k, with v = `h_v[k]` and its floats at `v * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`, `h_p[k]` is the sum over its four joints of `weight * linalg::point(pal[joint], position)` and `h_n[k]` the unit sum of `weight * linalg::qrotate(mquat(pal[joint]), normal)`.

`collide(i int, s f32)` pushes point i out of the body near it. For each of its two capsules z that exists: with `A = pos(motion::world[c_a[z]])`, `B = pos(motion::world[c_b[z]])`, `ab = B - A`, `t = clamp01(dot3(x - A, ab) / max(dot3(ab, ab), 0.000001))`, `c = A + ab * t`, `r = (c_r0[z] + (c_r1[z] - c_r0[z]) * t + 0.006) * s`, `d = x - c`, `l = length3(d)`: when `l < r`, set `x = c + (if l > 0.000001 { d / l } else { unit3(p_anc[i] - c) }) * r`. For each of its two contacts h that exists and lies within `0.07 * s` of x: with `e = dot3(x - h_p[h], h_n[h])`, when `e < 0.004 * s` move x by `h_n[h] * (0.004 * s - e)`. Store x back into `p_x[i]`.

`tether(i int, s f32)` keeps point i within its tether length of its tether point: when `p_tie[i] >= 0`, with `e = p_x[i] - p_x[p_tie[i]]`, `l = length3(e)` and `m = p_tl[i] * s * 1.02`, when `l > m` set `p_x[i] = p_x[p_tie[i]] + e * (m / l)`.

`limit(i int, s f32)` keeps point i within its max distance of its anchor: `e = p_x[i] - p_anc[i]`, `l = length3(e)`, `m = p_md[i] * s`; when `l > m`, `p_x[i] = p_anc[i] + (if l > 0.000001 { e * (m / l) } else { e })`.

`rotations(its int)` finds each point's rotation by shape matching with its neighbours (the method of Müller et al. 2016): for each point i, build the three columns of `A = sum over neighbours j of (p_x[j] - p_x[i]) * transpose(p_rest[j] - p_rest[i])`, that is column c is `sum (p_x[j] - p_x[i]) * (p_rest[j] - p_rest[i]).c` for c in x, y, z; then `its` times: with `q = p_q[i]`, `r0 = qrotate(q, v3(1, 0, 0))`, `r1 = qrotate(q, v3(0, 1, 0))`, `r2 = qrotate(q, v3(0, 0, 1))`, `om = (cross3(r0, a0) + cross3(r1, a1) + cross3(r2, a2)) * (1 / (abs(dot3(r0, a0) + dot3(r1, a1) + dot3(r2, a2)) + 0.000000001))`, `w = length3(om)`; stop when w < 0.000000001; else `p_q[i] = linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(linalg::qaxis(om / w, w), q))`.

`cloth_step(dt f32)` returns when `npts == 0`, or when the placement scale `s = length3(s3d::items[item].place.c0.xyz)` is below 0.001 (no pose from the page yet). Call `anchors()`. When `!ready || dt <= 0`: set every `p_x` and `p_prev` to its anchor; when `!ready`, set every `p_q` to `mquat(motion::world[p_node[i]])` composed after nothing (just that), run `rotations(20)`, and set `ready = true`. Otherwise, with `h = min(dt, 1 / 30)` and 3 substeps of `hs = h / 3`: each substep, for every point: when `p_im[i] == 0`, `p_prev[i] = p_x[i]` and `p_x[i] = p_anc[i]`; else `v = (p_x[i] - p_prev[i]) * p_damp[i]`, `p_prev[i] = p_x[i]`, `p_x[i] = p_x[i] + v + (v3(0, -9.81, 0) * p_grav[i] + wind) * (s * hs * hs)`. Then 7 iterations of: every edge with `wa = p_im[e_a]`, `wb = p_im[e_b]`, `sw = wa + wb`, skipping when sw is 0: `d = p_x[b] - p_x[a]`, `l = length3(d)`, skipping when l < 0.000000001; `c = d * ((l - e_l[k] * s) / l * e_k[k] / sw)`; `p_x[a] = p_x[a] + c * wa`; `p_x[b] = p_x[b] - c * wb`; then every point with `p_im[i] > 0`: `tether(i, s)`, `limit(i, s)` and `collide(i, s)` (the body wins over the swing limit). After the substeps run `rotations(3)`.
Finally pose the joints: for each point i with node n and parent `pn = gltf::nodes[n].parent` (skip when pn < 0): `wp = motion::world[pn]`, `iw = linalg::inverse(wp)`, `ps = length3(wp.c0.xyz)`; call `s3d::set_node(item, n, linalg::point(iw, p_x[i]), linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(qconj(mquat(wp)), p_q[i])), v3(s / ps, s / ps, s / ps))`.

`cloth_stats(out f32[:])` reports how each garment behaves in the current pose, for graders: `out[0] = ngar`; for each group g, at `1 + 4 g`: the number of its points, its flare and the number and deepest of its points inside the body. Flare: with `c = pos(motion::world[pelvis])` (the node `gltf::find_node(cl_asset, "pelvis")`, a global index; report flare 1 when it is missing), over the garment's points with `p_y < 0.15`, the mean horizontal distance (x and z) of `p_x` from c divided by the mean horizontal distance of `p_anc` from c (1 when there are none or the second mean is 0): above 1 the hem stands out further than the skinned body would carry it. Inside: a point is inside when it is inside one of its two linked caps (its distance from the cap's segment, as in `collide` with the placement scale s but without the 0.006 margin, is below the radius) or behind one of its contacts within 0.07 s (`dot3(x - h_p, h_n) < 0`); count those points and keep the deepest such depth (in meters, divided by s).


`cloth_wind(x f32, y f32, z f32)` sets `wind`.